first  background to the g7 meeting back in  2015  countries agreed to keep human-induced  global warming to  two degrees preferably 1.5 degrees above  pre-industrial levels  and as you can see on the climate show  dashboard scientists estimate  that we are currently at around 1.2  degrees and that number of course is  ticking up  all the time now the warming is caused  by burning fossil fuels which produce  greenhouse gases including carbon  dioxide  and earlier this week it was revealed  that in may  levels of co2 in the atmosphere reached  their highest level  since measurements began more than 60  years ago a monthly average  of just over 419 parts per million was  recorded  at the moana lao atmosphere baseline  observatory in hawaii now ppm  is the concentration of carbon in the  atmosphere that actually intensifies the  greenhouse effect  and warms our planet well scientists say  the only way out  of all of this is for countries but  especially the industrialized nations to  really reduce their emissions  but what chance is there of that were  with me now as our political editor  beth rigby we're keeping told beth don't  we that the climate is so important  at the g7 summit here but there's so  much else for the  politicians to be discussing what  will people be hoping that when sunday  comes and the leaders  leave will have been achieved when it  comes to the climate  well look i think the fast thing to say  is in his opening remarks boris johnson  said that he thought this was a huge  opportunity to tackle  climate change he says as a g7 we are  united  in our vision for a cleaner greener  world and if you look across  the g7 countries all have long term  targets to reach  net zero emissions by 2050 all have  confirmed their commitment  to holding temperature rises no more  than above 1.5  degrees celsius pre-industrial levels as  you were talking about  which is a lower end of the target set  in the paris agreements in 2015  so there is a lot of goodwill on the  part of the g7 but the big  elephant in the room is it's not  industrialized countries who are  set to emit most low carbon emissions in  the future more than 90 of emissions in  the next  two decades are expected to come from  emerging economies and that's why  activists and campaigners  want to see richer countries actually  help poorer countries to deal  with getting cleaner and greener there  was a pledge in 2009  for a hundred billion dollars a year  from public or private  sources to be given paid out to emerging  economies  to make their countries greener that was  a target which was meant to be hit  in 2020 will that be dusted down  at this summit and and the other big  elephant  in the room is china it's all very well  for the g7  to make commitments but as angela merkel  the outgoing german chancellor  said today we need everyone in the world  we want to work together particularly in  areas of climate change and biodiversity  we will never achieve solutions there  without china so again there's lots of  good will there's lots of talk  president biden has accelerated the u.s  carbon emission targets boris johnson is  leading the world in terms of cutting  emissions with the most ambitious  targets by 2030  and then 2035 but let's see what happens  at the end of the week because  really it's about developing economies  and what that comes down to is that the  g7 can make  targets but what they need to do is bind  in the g20  of course it's all linked to covid as  well isn't it because so many of those  developing countries  are having to spend their money on  dealing with the pandemic rather than  worrying about  about the climate and that sort of  brings me on to my next question beth um  boris johnson obviously hosting the  summit here he's also going to be  hosting cop 26 in november so the  pressure really is  on him isn't it to get results here  before the end of the year  yeah that's right the big challenge for  him is exactly that which is what 